A||N||12||TwisT||4-speed vs. 5-speed||||5||
Z||000000||TwisT||01-07-2000||09:05 PM||||OK gang...  need some input here.  I've owned a 4-speed GT before;  but hadn't noticed until last summer how much faster the 4-speed feels compared to my current 5-speed.  <P>The 4-speed equiped '86 GT that I drove blazed the tires in typical muscle-car style,  while my '88 GT doesn't.  I've heard that the 4-speed has a 4.10 gear ratio,  and the 5-speed is geared taller.  But WOW...  what a difference.<P>Can anyone else relate?  What are your experiences?<P>||||reg||5
Z||000001||88formula||01-07-2000||09:40 PM||||I own an 88 formula with a five speed and a 3.66 axle ratio and it readily burns the tires on command in traditional American muscle car style. I know that the four-cylinder model was offered with that lower axle ratio that you mention, but I don't know about the six.<p>[This message has been edited by 88formula (edited 01-07-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||william_thorniii||01-07-2000||09:54 PM||||The infamous M19 has a 4.10 axle ratio with a 3.32 overall.  This is the lowest it gets.  This was only an option in 84.  In 85 (M17) the ratio with the 6 jumped up to 3.65 and in the latter part of 86 the 5 spd came out as 3.61 and stayed there through the rest of production.  The numbers say that there isn't much of a jump, but I think that it usually feels greater than it is too.  Here are the indiv. gear ratios (M19) 3.53, 1.95, 1.24, .81 (M17) 3.31, 1.95, 1.24, .81 (MG2:Getrag) 3.50, 2.05, 1.38, .94, .72, (MG1:Izusu) 3.73, 2.04, 1.45, 1.07, .74<P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||3
Z||000003||TwisT||01-08-2000||01:36 AM||||...hmmm.  I'm wondering about the trade-offs between using the 4 or 5.  Five,  obviously,  gives you that nice 5th gear which is nice on the highway.  The four gives excellent acceleration on the bottom end,  but revs higher at highway speeds.<P>This M19...  this can be used in place of a 5 speed car,  correct?||||reg||1
Z||000004||SCCA FIERO||01-08-2000||02:39 AM||||My friend put a 4 speed M19 in place of his 4 speed in his 86 GT. I thought the later 4 speeds were notchy and took a strong left leg to press the clutch in. That M19 was almost impossible to shift even after you struggled to get the pedal down. I tell ya though, that Crane cam, gutted cat, ported exhaust manifolds and those 225-50ZR-16 tires were screaming for mercy. <P>I have the Isuzu running behind a V6 and that 3.73 first gear is no sloutch! I can ignite the rears upon demand. It's a smooth tranny and pedal effort is almost non-existant. Usually I have a grin form gear to gear. I have also buried my 120 mph speedo in 4th gear.||||reg||1
Z||000005||william_thorniii||01-09-2000||02:49 AM||||SCCA,<P>I can assure you that my M17 is not any more difficult to shift than my college roomates Honda Accord.  Perhaps you and your friend should examine his master/slave cylinders and linkage.  I do agree with you that it is notchy.  I understand that is indicative of all 80's US built transmissions.<P>William T. Thorn, III<P>Yes, both the M17 and the M19 bolt up to 5-spd equipped Fiero's<P>||||reg||1
Z||000006||fiero56||01-09-2000||01:24 PM||||Pressure plate springs could also be a factor. There was a thread in the last several months about very light clutch pedal after installing the Centerforce d/f clutch.||||reg||1
Z||000007||SCCA FIERO||01-09-2000||02:37 PM||||  I owned an 86 SE for 10 years with the M17 4 speed and the pedal effort was pretty stiff. I did finally discover the pedal was bent and replaced that. I must admit that it shifted 400% better but the pedal effort was still there. Every 4 speed I have driven has a stiff pedal, but that may be because the thing is indestructible. <P>  I have driven a few Getrags and the pedal will fall to the floor if you look at it hard enough and it stirs the gears very nicely. <P>  Then there is the Isuzu. It may not be able to handle the HP of the V6 atleast that's what I hear. As soon as I find the breaking point I will let you know.  One season of autoxing already on it and countless burnouts and the usual badboy stuff.||||reg||6
Z||000008||william_thorniii||01-09-2000||06:03 PM||||I hear that the Isuzu has other gear sets that can be swapped out.  Is there any truth in that?  <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||5
Z||000009||SCCA FIERO||01-09-2000||06:45 PM||||I have heard the same thing,so I imagine it is true. I also imagine it's expensive.||||reg||1
Z||000010||ChadMan||01-10-2000||11:47 AM||||So you've got a year on your 88 coupe 4-banger-to-V6 conversion, SCCA?  How many miles have you put on it?  Do you use it as a daily driver, or just for racing and pleasure driving?  Any electrical glitches after the swap?<P>As the owner of a sharp but underpowered 88 Coupe, I like to keep tabs on how your swap went.  Hopefully after I buy a house I can afford to start playing around with my Fiero.||||reg||1
Z||000011||SCCA FIERO||01-10-2000||03:00 PM||||I do have an 88 Coupe with an 86 2.8 V6 in it. I did this swap about 7 or 8 months ago and drove it daily all summer including autocrossing and a trip to the strip with 5 passes before I got put out. I did have some electrical problems, but I installed the rear harness from the 86 and solved it(I pushed the brake pedal and no brake lights came on but the interior lights and radiator fan came on). The hardest part was the cooling lines. The 86 lines are totally different from 88(I still can't get my heater to work, but since I parked it as soon as it got cold I left it alone). Also, the motor mount from 86-88 is different, I had to drill holes in mine to set it back 1 3/4 inches. I did have the complete running V6 car which helped alot. I also installed the 86 dash cluster and dash.||||reg||3
Z||000012||HF6582||01-11-2000||06:22 PM||||I don't know if they are still available and this is all of the info I have, but maybe it will help.<BR>Here is how it is listed in an old GM parts book.<P>Transmission Internal Quick Change Gear Set For GM 4spd    49 Ratios Available<BR>Webster Gears, Inc.<P>No phone # or address listed.<P>Scott||||reg||1
